That would be me.  Understanding how fat works in the body is painfully
complex.  It's important to realize that very low fat diets can be extremely
dangerous.  In certain people, for example, dramatically lowering fat intake
can send cholesterol levels through the roof--the reverse of what you'd
expect.    
 
Fats are essential for many functions.  Vitamin A, for instance, (which
seems to actually consist of between 17 and 22 different chemicals at
different stages of use in the body) requires certain fat interactions to
work.  Since low levels of vitamin A are tightly correlated to chronic
disease, you can see the importance of getting the right kinds and
sufficient amounts of fat.  
 
I'm not saying that cutting fat out of your diet never works; just be sure
you know what you're doing.  You don't want to exchange heart disease for
cancer.  I recommend "Fats and Oils," by Udo Erasmus for a good foundation
in this area.
 
