Date:     Fri, 29 Jan 93 11:32:01 GMT
From: starr@genie.slhs.udel.edu
Subject: ECON: Perfect Competition

Some assumptions about the economic theory of markets were recently brought
up by an apparent newcomer (welcome!), Dave Worley.  Specifically, he
claimed that most of the problems cited by statists with alleged free
markets were in cases in which there actually weren't free markets.  I was
unsatisfied with his discussion of the subject, however, and believe
that the underlying assumptions need to be exposed to sunlight, the best
disinfectant.

In the early 20th century, some economists came up with something they
called "perfect competition."  David Friedman may correct me on this, but
I recall it to be a theoretical construct including "perfect information,"
meaning no search costs, no externalities, ease of entry and exit, and
many firms competing for many customers.  There may be more to these.

It's very important to notice that these aren't conclusions derived from
empirical observation or from axiomatic premises.  They are assumptions.
Economists who employ them presume them.

Ah, yes, another assumption is that in such a market the price of goods will
approach marginal cost.

This became used as a standard for measuring "efficiency."  Any market which
didn't meet these conditions was held to be "inefficient," and a good
candidate for statist intervention.  Such inefficiencies were called
"market failures."

Some of the assumptions are inevitably false, however.  Nobel economist
(1974) Friedrich Hayek's lesson is that no statist can know enough about
such things as time and place for there ever to be perfect information.
Nobel economist Ronald Coase (1992) came up with the concept of transaction
costs as well to cover the costs of searching for information among other
things.  Omniscience is impossible.

The prohibition of externalities is mysterious to me.  It seems to be
completely arbitrary, but probably isn't.

Ease of entry and exit varies and bears on the question of monopoly, as
does the number of firms (or, rather, the market share of firms).  Markets
in which one firm has large market share (such as US Steel at one time,
Alcoa at another, and Ma Bell before its breakup) and/or in which there
are large economies of scale, startup costs, etc., have been called
monopolized.  Anti-trust proceedings have broken up firms in order to
better approximate the standard of perfect competition.

There have been at least two major problems with this.  One is that little
attention has been paid to the fact that statism often is the factor that
determines how easy it is to enter or exit a market.  Adam Smith wrote of
how monopoly franchises were handed out by the King of England to favored
courtiers.  The way to make one's way in the world was to go to court,
present oneself as a gentleman, ingratiate oneself with an accident of
birth, get a monopoly, force others to stay out of that market, and live
happily ever after.  This ended when a particularly lazy King came to
the throne who was too busy having fun to hand out monopolies.

Similarly, the US Post Office has a monopoly on mail service.  It was
challenged by Lysander Spooner during the Jacksonian Era of US history,
after the Bank of the United States had been abolished (thus making
abolition of the Post Office plausible), but he failed.  The American
Letter Mail Company that Spooner started to compete with the US Post
Office was outlawed, his innovations adopted, and postal rates raised.

Few coercive monopolies that I know of have been put out of business by
competition.  Many natural ones (by the standard of their having a large
market share, not that of being the only firm in the market; the latter are
too few) have been.  After Charles Schwab left US Steel for Bethlehem, he
reduced US Steel's market share from about 90% to about half in short
order.

In modern times, it has taken anti-trust proceedings to break up monopolies,
such as the breakup of the telephone company.  However, the problem with
anti-trust law is that it has often been used by monopolists in an anti-
competitive fashion.  One of the first companies subjected to it was that
of Henry Hill, the Great Northern Railroad, the only railroad at the time
that relied solely on consumer demand and didn't accept the subsidy
provided by the Federal government on a per-mile of track-laid basis.  His
railroad didn't exploit coolie labor, didn't meander around to get higher
subsidies, and made a profit by encouraging marginal settlement of the
northeast.  Anti-trust was also used against labor unions to break strikes
which were held to be in restraint of trade.

Antri-trust lends itself to such persecution because it's inherently
arbitrary.  You can be prosecuted for raising prices, for lowering prices,
or both at the same time (this has happened).  How many firms must there
be for a market to be competitive?  How easy must entry be?  There are
no principled answers to these questions that I know of.

The last question is that of public goods, which deserves better than I
can give it here.  Suffice to say that if I must have some money taken from
me in order to save me from having to pay a higher price, then this is
acceptable.  Otherwise, it is theft.

