I don't think Garrett is using the same definition of socialism that I
would (state ownership of the means of production).  He seems to be
referring more to a "welfare" or "giveaway" state.

It's true, though, that in the U.S., much of the movement away from our
original relatively libertarian government in the late 19th and early
20th centuries was due to popular discontent.  There was fear that if
such measures were not taken, just such a popular revolt might occur.
The fear of communism was high in those days, especially after the
Russian revolution, with the fear that a communist revolt might occur
in the U.S.  So to a certain extent the statist measures that we rue so
greatly really were calculated bribes to the working class by the
frightened monied interests.

This analysis is based on the book "Crisis and Leviathan" (author not
handy), which shows how a series of crises (several panics, the
depression, both world wars) each ratcheted the size and power of the
federal government upward to previously unheard-of levels.

I think the lesson is that the real key to a successful non-coercive
society is education of the people about the costs and benefits of
various measures.  Many of the topics we have discussed here could help
with that process.  Robin's idea futures could provide graphic evidence
of expert opinion on economic growth rates under alternative economic
systems.  And Eli's point about the need for new structures to make
market's work "better" is relevant as well.  Private unemployment
insurance would be an example of something which could take the place of
much government charity, and might reduce the clamor for coercive aid.

Hal

