Subject: ECON: Commodity Futures

Perry is technically incorrect when he calls the various futures
markets "zero-sum games."  They, and all other forms of
-institutional- betting are actually -negative- sum games.  The
negative component comes from the transaction costs, aka broker's
commissions, house percentage, etc.
 
If you bet with friends on the superbowl, poker game, coin flip, etc.,
-that's- a zero-sum game.  As a group, all the participants take away
just what they brought (but redistributed).
 
In -institutional- betting, the participants as a group take away
-less- than they bring; the difference finances the institution.
 
For those who are looking for tips on how to play the commodity
market, I can offer the following negative advice derived from sad
experience.  Do NOT open a "managed" account with a self-styled
"commodity guru" like Dennis Turner (is he still around?).

