> then the people would revolt.  ...  
> socialism gives everyone a couple of cards to start with.  This
> however unequal "equal opportunity" makes it a lot harder to revolt.

Hal Finney responds:
>He seems to be referring more to a "welfare" or "giveaway" state.
>It's true, though, that in the U.S., much of the movement away from our
>original relatively libertarian government in the late 19th and early
>20th centuries was due to popular discontent.  There was fear that if
>such measures were not taken, just such a popular revolt might occur. ...
>I think the lesson is that the real key to a successful non-coercive
>society is education of the people about the costs and benefits of
>various measures. 

Education may be enough, but we should consider the possibility that
it may not be.  In (informal, even implicit) negotiations over law and
economic policy, a credible capability and willingness to revolt gives
a group negotiating leverage, which they can translate into
concessions from other groups.  While it is probability not in fact in
that group's interest to use their leverage to gain state intervention
in the workings of the economy (and this is where education helps), it
may in fact be in their interest to use that leverage to gain direct
transfers of wealth.  If so, appeals to non-coercion axioms are just
so much rhetorical smoke to convince them to give in in negotiations.
If they hold firm, then you may be better off making a compromise deal,
and transferring some (not all) wealth.

What we could use is better institutions for us all to guage the
actual negotiating value of the threat to revolt, so that we can
create a "Shelling point" for a wealth transfer amount.  This is to
avoid the fear (described well by David Friedman in his paper on the
subject posted here a few months ago) that once you give in to any
wealth transfer now, next year they'll insist on much more.

Folks who know me can probably imagine the sort of thing I'll propose.
Create betting markets on the chance of a revolt, and the magnitude of
the consequences for different groups, all conditional on various
levels of wealth transfer.  These betting markets should be far away,
so as not to be themselves threatened by a revolt here, and markets in
the long-term consequences of giving in so much now would be
particularly important.

Those from whom blood is sucked could then more rationally choose
between more blood loss or a higher chance of a fatal bite.  Cold
reality, but it doesn't pay to ignore reality.

Robin Hanson



